WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.600
When a model needs more than
one GPU, you decide how to cut it.

2
00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:08.550
Cut it the wrong way, and the links
between GPUs become the bottleneck.

3
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.010
Picture a model as a stack of layers.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.620
There are two basic cuts.

5
00:00:12.692 --> 00:00:15.912
Tensor parallelism
cuts through every layer.

6
00:00:15.972 --> 00:00:21.532
Each GPU holds a slice of every weight
matrix, so the GPUs share each layer's

7
00:00:21.532 --> 00:00:23.732
weight reads and math.

8
00:00:23.792 --> 00:00:28.082
After every layer, they combine
partial results in an all-reduce.

9
00:00:28.232 --> 00:00:31.872
That traffic needs fast
NVLink inside one node.

10
00:00:31.932 --> 00:00:34.092
The payoff is the lowest latency.

11
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.540
Pipeline parallelism cuts between layers.

12
00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.140
Each GPU holds whole layers as one stage.

13
00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:47.460
GPUs talk only at the stage boundaries, so
the traffic suits slower links between nodes.

14
00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:53.270
The cost is that stages wait on each
other, and per-request latency rises.

15
00:00:53.655 --> 00:00:55.645
Production systems combine the two.

16
00:00:55.825 --> 00:01:01.025
Tensor parallelism runs inside each
node, and pipeline stages span nodes.

17
00:01:01.135 --> 00:01:06.105
TP8PP2 means eight-way tensor
parallelism in each of two stages.

18
00:01:06.217 --> 00:01:06.987
Pause and choose.

19
00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:14.257
Case one: a model fits on the eight GPUs of
one node, and users want the fastest responses.

20
00:01:14.357 --> 00:01:19.137
Case two: a dense model needs
two nodes linked over InfiniBand.

21
00:01:19.137 --> 00:01:20.817
Which plan fits each case?
